{"authored" [{"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients in total, counting the patient with the first positive result is{{field-1}}." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/review.json" "file_sha256" "edbebc4171d3e6c539d698023def000f62c2745d1ab4c690ed3d88f06dc0ff3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "4c33df14d2a74b353917b2bb4d41b207c24c316b3bec0e525fb7ec1893a53775" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/verification.json" "basis" 2432 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for 0.833 and the revealed source solution establish the intended event T>6, with the first positive included. Literal failures-before wording can instead give 0.808. The clarification explicitly states the total-trials counting convention. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients in total, counting the patient with the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHere $X$ counts all tested patients, including the first positive. Thus $X>6$ means that the first six test results are negative. The original phrase “before seeing the first positive” was ambiguous; this study question uses the total-trials convention." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/review.json" "file_sha256" "edbebc4171d3e6c539d698023def000f62c2745d1ab4c690ed3d88f06dc0ff3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "4c33df14d2a74b353917b2bb4d41b207c24c316b3bec0e525fb7ec1893a53775" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/verification.json" "basis" 2432 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for 0.833 and the revealed source solution establish the intended event T>6, with the first positive included. Literal failures-before wording can instead give 0.808. The clarification explicitly states the total-trials counting convention. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-332355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.833"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/review.json" "file_sha256" "edbebc4171d3e6c539d698023def000f62c2745d1ab4c690ed3d88f06dc0ff3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "4c33df14d2a74b353917b2bb4d41b207c24c316b3bec0e525fb7ec1893a53775" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-332355/verification.json" "basis" 2432 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for 0.833 and the revealed source solution establish the intended event T>6, with the first positive included. Literal failures-before wording can instead give 0.808. The clarification explicitly states the total-trials counting convention. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd9582